      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 John Wiley & Sons, Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "PURPOSE",
            "content": "The aim of this study was to assess the efficacy and safety of ivabradine (Iva) noninferiority to atenolol (Aten) in Chinese patients with chronic stable angina pectoris."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "In this double-blind, double-dummy trial, patients with symptomatic angina pectoris and positive exercise tolerance test were randomized into the Iva [5 or 7.5\u2009mg bis in die (BID)] or Aten group (12.5 or 25\u2009mg BID) according to computer-generated random numbers for 12\u2009weeks."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "One hundred and sixty-eight patients were randomized to the Iva group and 166 to the Aten group. In a full analysis set, increases in the total exercise duration (TED) were 54.3\u2009±\u2009120.1\u2009seconds with Iva 5\u2009mg and 58.8\u2009±\u2009114.7\u2009seconds with Aten 12.5\u2009mg at the fourth week, and at the 12th week, TED improved by 84.1\u2009±\u2009130.5\u2009seconds with Iva and 77.8\u2009±\u2009126.6\u2009seconds with Aten (95%CI: -21.4-34.1\u2009seconds, p\u2009=\u20090.0011 for noninferiority). The analysis of per protocol set yielded similar results (95%CI: -31.4-33.0\u2009seconds, p\u2009=\u20090.0131 for noninferiority). Heart rate was reduced in both groups at rest and during peak exercise. There were small, nonsignificant differences in the number of adverse events between the two groups (66 in Iva and 73 in Aten, p\u2009>\u20090.05). Nine patients (5.42%) were reported to develop phosphenes/luminous phenomena and blurred vision in the Iva group (p\u2009=\u20090.0035)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Iva is effective in reducing heart rates and improving exercise capacity and noninferior to Aten in Chinese patients with chronic stable angina pectoris. Iva is well tolerated and safe."
          }
        ]
      },
